---
import Document from '../layouts/Document.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, HOSTING_REGION } from '../lib/legal';
import { withBase } from '../lib/locale';
import { GITHUB_REPO_URL } from '../lib/download';

const title = 'Privacy Policy';
const description =
  'What TechTok stores about you, who processes it, how long it is kept, and how to delete it.';
---

<Document title={title} description={description} subpath="privacy/">
  <p>
    TechTok is a reading app for technology and science news. It shows short summaries of articles
    published by other people, and links out to the originals. This page explains exactly what the
    app stores about you and what you can do about it.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is written to be read, not to be survived. If something here is unclear, ask — the whole
    service is <a href={GITHUB_REPO_URL} rel="noopener">open source</a>, so the answer is always
    checkable against the code.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>

  <p>
    TechTok is an independent project run by a single developer, who is the data controller for the
    purposes of the GDPR. Contact: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app stores about you</h2>

  <p>
    You sign in with Google, and TechTok keeps one record per account. That record contains only the
    following.
  </p>

  <div class="doc-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it exists</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Your Google account identifier</td>
          <td>Identifies your account. It is what links your reading history to you across devices.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your email address and display name</td>
          <td>Comes from Google when you sign in, so the app can show you which account you are using.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your time zone</td>
          <td>Captured once at sign-in, so that daily usage counters reset at your local midnight rather than at midnight UTC.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your language, followed topics, and muted sources</td>
          <td>Your preferences. They decide what the feed shows you and in which language.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Which articles you have read, and when</td>
          <td>Stops the feed showing you the same article twice, and powers the History screen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your bookmarks</td>
          <td>The Saved screen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A per-topic read count</td>
          <td>A ranking signal — topics you read more get a small boost in the feed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your plan and daily usage counters</td>
          <td>Everyone is currently on the free plan. The counters exist so the service can apply fair daily limits.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Some things stay on your phone and are never sent anywhere: articles cached for offline reading,
    and your light/dark theme choice.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app does not do</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>There is <strong>no advertising</strong>, and no advertising SDK in the app.</li>
    <li>
      There is <strong>no advertising or tracking SDK</strong>, and nothing profiles you across
      other apps or websites. The app does send crash reports and a small amount of anonymous
      performance data to Sentry so that breakages can be found and fixed — see below.
    </li>
    <li>Your data is <strong>never sold</strong>, and never shared for anyone else's marketing.</li>
    <li>There is no access to your location, contacts, photos, camera, or microphone.</li>
    <li>There is no advertising identifier, and no cross-app or cross-site tracking.</li>
    <li>There are no push notifications, so no notification token is stored.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who else is involved</h2>

  <p>Running the service means a small number of other companies process data on its behalf.</p>

  <div class="doc-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Who</th>
          <th scope="col">What they handle</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Google</td>
          <td>Sign-in. Google confirms who you are and provides your email and name. Google's own privacy policy governs your Google account.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Amazon Web Services</td>
          <td>Hosting — the servers, file storage, and content delivery. Everything runs in {HOSTING_REGION}.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Neon</td>
          <td>
            The database. Every record described above — your account, your preferences, your
            reading history and your bookmarks — is stored in a Neon Postgres database hosted in
            the EU.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sentry</td>
          <td>
            Crash reporting. When the app crashes or a request fails, it sends the error, a stack
            trace, and technical details about the device (model, operating system version, app
            version) to Sentry's European servers. It does not receive your email address, your
            name, your reading history, or anything you type into the app.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Expo</td>
          <td>Delivers app updates. When the app starts, it asks Expo whether a newer version is available; that request carries app and device version information, not your identity.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>OpenRouter</td>
          <td>
            Produces the summaries and translations. It receives <strong>article text from public
            news feeds only</strong> — never your account details and never anything about what you
            personally read. Summarising happens when an article is published, not when you open it.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    When you tap <strong>Read original</strong>, your browser opens the publisher's own website. From
    that moment the publisher's privacy policy applies, not this one.
  </p>

  <h2>Where your data is kept</h2>

  <p>
    All account data is stored inside the EU — application data in {HOSTING_REGION}, the database
    in Neon's EU region, and crash reports on Sentry's EU servers. Google, Expo, Neon, Sentry and
    OpenRouter are US-headquartered companies, so their processing may involve transfers outside
    the EU under the standard safeguards those companies publish.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>

  <p>
    Your account and reading history are kept <strong>until you delete them</strong>. There is no
    automatic expiry — if you keep using TechTok, your history keeps growing, and if you delete your
    account it goes immediately. Cached article content, which is not personal data, is discarded
    after about 90 days.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>

  <p>
    Under the GDPR you can ask for a copy of your data, ask for it to be corrected, ask for it to be
    deleted, object to how it is used, or complain to your national data protection authority. The
    fastest of these is deletion, which you can do yourself, immediately, without asking anyone.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Deleting your account</strong> removes your record and your entire reading history and
      bookmarks. See <a href={withBase('delete-account/')}>how to delete your account</a> — it works
      from inside the app, and also by email if you have already uninstalled it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For anything else on that list, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>

  <p>
    TechTok is not directed at children and does not knowingly collect data from anyone under 13. If
    you believe a child has an account, email the address above and it will be removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>

  <p>
    If what the app stores ever changes, this page changes with it, and the date at the top moves.
    Paid subscriptions are planned but not yet available; when they arrive, this page will be updated
    before they go live.
  </p>
</Document>
